@startuml fig-http-methods-idempotency
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam defaultFontName Arial
skinparam defaultFontSize 11
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam roundcorner 12
left to right direction

title HTTP Methods — Safety, Idempotency and Cacheability

card "**GET**\nRetrieve a resource\n\nSafe: Yes\nIdempotent: Yes\nCacheable: Yes" as GET #C8E6C9

card "**POST**\nCreate / submit\n\nSafe: No\nIdempotent: Not guaranteed\nCacheable: Under explicit HTTP conditions" as POST #FFCDD2

card "**PUT**\nFull replacement\n\nSafe: No\nIdempotent: Yes\nCacheable: No" as PUT #BBDEFB

card "**PATCH**\nPartial update\n\nSafe: No\nIdempotent: Not guaranteed*\nCacheable: Under RFC 5789 conditions" as PATCH #FFF9C4

card "**DELETE**\nRemove a resource\n\nSafe: No\nIdempotent: Yes\nCacheable: No" as DELETE #FFE0B2

card "**OPTIONS**\nCapabilities query\n\nSafe: Yes\nIdempotent: Yes\nCacheable: No" as OPTIONS #ECEFF1

GET -[hidden]right-> POST
POST -[hidden]right-> PUT
PUT -[hidden]right-> PATCH
PATCH -[hidden]right-> DELETE
DELETE -[hidden]right-> OPTIONS

legend bottom
  Safe: the client does not request a state-changing effect.
  Logging and similar incidental side effects are permitted.
  Idempotent: repeating an identical request has the same
  intended server effect as making it once; responses may differ.
  *PATCH can be designed to be idempotent, but the method does not guarantee it.
  PATCH caching requires explicit freshness and a matching Content-Location;
  the stored representation can answer subsequent GET/HEAD, not PATCH.
  Proxies and load balancers may retry idempotent methods automatically.
endlegend
@enduml
